Ranked, falsifiable hypotheses before testing:

1. The 237 panel was produced because the builder accepted a checkpoint narrower than the bound v1 encoder’s full 240-vector and sliced off three trailing globals; replacing slicing with exact equality should make that historical build fail and a 240 checkpoint pass.
2. Q1-v2’s width is exactly 236 because its encoder removes two member fields and C3 concatenates two ordered pooled member summaries, yielding `240 − 2×2 = 236`; source inspection and a real v2 context must agree.
3. The physical content is independent of the nominated checkpoint, so using real v1/v2 checkpoints should change only encoded state vectors (plus the authorized provenance flag/metadata), while all catalogue identities, outcomes, and reference endpoints remain byte-value equal.
4. If the v2 feature corpus is not usable by the original producer binding, a schema-specific encoder/source binding—not more truncation—will be required; the fail-closed build should expose that cleanly.

I’m proceeding with those tests now; the real-scorer invocation is the exact end-to-end red/green loop.
