The exact-source discovery authenticated both physics and view SHA-256 sidecars before accepting each anchor. Exactly the first 20 available entries in global generation order were used: global indices 000–019, comprising steps 0–5 for `nearest-eligible`, `stay-if-possible`, and `random-masked`, followed by step 6 for `nearest-eligible` and `stay-if-possible`. This is a prefix, not a selection. Their world is `V025_PROBE/world/1`, date is `2026-01-07`, and world seed is `5261619120743994529`.

### Fixed point and 10-second incumbent

The fixed-point traversal is deterministic cyclic first improvement:

1. users in ascending user-ID order;
2. each user's declared legal options in their declared order;
3. candidates physically evaluated in ordered microbatches of 16;
4. accept the first strict guarded-`F` improvement;
5. terminate only after a complete final pass finds no strict improvement.

PANELCEIL's completed authenticated receipt was available and was reused for global anchors 000–011. Its file SHA-256 is `52400cf180cd55f4b004be785ba4ff8021e09e38eba20cec7321fbad9999cf7d`, its internal receipt SHA-256 is `09e3e9c5e01f96bb879170ee1c31f752448f33fa6772284fabe8cbbea7fbceca`, and its path is `/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ceiling30-ws/.scratch/panelceil/panelceil-receipt.json`. A post-build join verified that all 12 reused certified and 10-second endpoints match PANELCEIL exactly on bits, joules, served count, and rate-target-attained count.

PANELCEIL covers only 12 anchors. For global anchors 012–019 the producer ran the same first-improvement implementation and required `termination_certificate.complete == true`; all eight passed. The 10-second reference is the last accepted fully evaluated iterate whose acceptance time is at most `10.0` seconds, not the base by definition and not the terminal certificate result by timing accident. The certified and 10-second profile identities differ on two of the 20 anchors.

The construction receipt records 12 `PANELCEIL` reference sources, eight producer-computed sources, and complete fixed-point certificates for all 20.

